<h1>2.4 Output caching</h1>
<p class="lead">Output caching (.NET 7 and later) is the modern server-side response cache. <b>You</b> decide what is cached, not the client, and you can remove cached responses by tag when data changes.</p>

<div class="story" data-story="2.4"></div>


<details class="deep">
<summary>&#128214; Want to understand it deeply? Read the full explanation</summary>

<h2>Why a second response cache?</h2>
<p>Response caching follows HTTP rules made for browsers and proxies. That is great for interoperability, but awkward for protecting your own server: clients can bypass it, and you cannot remove an entry when the data changes. Output caching is configured on the server, for the server.</p>
<div class="card">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Response caching (2.3)</th><th>Output caching (2.4)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Configured by</b></td><td>HTTP headers (<code>Cache-Control</code>)</td><td>Server-side policies in code</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Client can bypass with no-cache</b></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Affects browser caching</b></td><td>Yes, sends <code>Cache-Control</code></td><td>No, browsers are not told anything</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Remove entries on demand</b></td><td>No</td><td>Yes, by tag (<code>EvictByTagAsync</code>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Many requests for the same missing entry</b></td><td>All run the action</td><td>Only one runs, the others wait for it (resource locking)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Storage</b></td><td>Memory only</td><td>Memory by default, can be Redis (<code>AddStackExchangeRedisOutputCache</code>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Vary by</b></td><td>Query keys, headers</td><td>Query, headers, route values, or any custom value</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2>How it is set up in this project</h2>
<p>A named policy in <code>Program.cs</code>:</p>
<pre><code>builder.Services.AddOutputCache(options =&gt;
    options.AddPolicy("Products", policy =&gt; policy
        .Expire(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30))
        .SetVaryByQuery("id")
        .Tag("products")));

app.UseRouting();
app.UseOutputCache();</code></pre>
<p>Used on the endpoint:</p>
<pre><code>[HttpGet("products")]
[OutputCache(PolicyName = "Products")]
public async Task&lt;IActionResult&gt; Get(int id)</code></pre>
<p>And removed by tag when you want, through <code>IOutputCacheStore</code>:</p>
<pre><code>await outputCacheStore.EvictByTagAsync("products", cancellationToken);</code></pre>

<h2>Tags: removing a group of responses</h2>
<p>Every response stored by the <code>Products</code> policy carries the tag <code>products</code>: product 1, product 2, and so on. Evicting the tag removes all of them at once. In a real app you call this right after updating a product, so nobody gets an old price. This is your first taste of <b>invalidation</b>, the subject of Level 6.</p>

<h2>Resource locking: protection against a stampede</h2>
<p>Imagine a cached response expires while 1,000 users are asking for it. Without protection, all 1,000 requests miss and run the same expensive query at the same moment. This is a <b>cache stampede</b>. Output caching locks by default: the first request runs the action, and the others wait and receive its result. Level 7 shows how to build this yourself.</p>

</details>

