<h2>Experiments: do these in order</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li><b>Cache it.</b> Click <em>Send request</em> twice. The first runs the action, the second is answered from the output cache.</li>
    <li><b>Client cannot bypass.</b> Tick <em>Client sends Cache-Control: no-cache</em> and send again. Still served from the cache. Compare this with lesson 2.3.</li>
    <li><b>Stale data and tags.</b> Click <em>Change price in database</em>, then <em>Send request</em>: the old price, because the response is cached for 30 seconds. Click <em>Evict tag "products"</em> and send again: the action runs and you see the new price.</li>
    <li><b>Stampede protection.</b> Click <em>Evict tag "products"</em> so the cache is empty, then <em>Send 10 requests at the same time</em>. All ten arrive together, but the action runs only <b>once</b>. The other nine waited for that one result.</li>
    <li><b>Expiry.</b> Wait 30 seconds and send again. The entry expired, so the action runs.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What you should take away</h2>
<div class="callout tip">
    <strong>Key points</strong>
    Output caching is the server-side response cache to use in modern ASP.NET Core. You control it with policies, clients cannot bypass it, you can evict by tag when data changes, and it stops stampedes by letting only one request rebuild a missing entry. It does not tell browsers to cache. Combine it with <code>Cache-Control</code> headers if you also want that.
</div>
<div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Common mistakes</strong>
    Caching responses that depend on the user (by default, requests with authentication are not cached, so do not change that without varying by user). Forgetting to evict after writes. Putting <code>UseOutputCache()</code> before <code>UseRouting()</code>, so attribute-based policies do not apply. With several servers, the default memory store gives each server its own cache, so evicting on one server does not evict on the others. A Redis store fixes that (Level 4).
</div>

